  and [1mexpiry is tested without sleeping[0m — the clock is a parameter, so "one millisecond past [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m" is a synchronous
  assertion. The CommonJS export also matches the convention already in [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, so it isn't a new pattern in this
  repo.

  [1mCases[0m — one file, [38;5;153mtest/identity.test.js[39m:

  [3mRound-trip:[0m [38;5;153mset[39m then [38;5;153mget[39m returns the userId; [38;5;153mget[39m with nothing stored returns [38;5;153mnull[39m.

  [3mRejecting bad records[0m (each asserts both [38;5;153mget() === null[39m [1mand[0m that the key was removed): unparseable JSON; valid JSON of
  the wrong shape; missing [38;5;153muserId[39m; empty-string [38;5;153muserId[39m; non-finite [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m.

  [3mExpiry:[0m valid one ms before [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m; [38;5;153mnull[39m one ms after, and the key is gone.

  [3mClearing:[0m [38;5;153mclear()[39m removes the key; [38;5;153mget()[39m after [38;5;153mclear()[39m is [38;5;153mnull[39m.

  [3mSubscribers:[0m [38;5;153monChange[39m fires on [38;5;153mset[39m and on [38;5;153mclear[39m; the returned unsubscribe stops delivery; a subscriber that throws
  doesn't prevent other subscribers from running or break [38;5;153mset()[39m.

  [3mStorage failure:[0m a fake whose [38;5;153msetItem[39m throws — [38;5;153mset()[39m returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m rather than propagating, and [38;5;153mget()[39m still returns
  the userId from the in-memory fallback. A fake whose [38;5;153mgetItem[39m throws — [38;5;153mget()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m rather than propagating.

  [1mNot tested:[0m the DOM submit handler in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — it needs jsdom, which is the dependency we're avoiding, and it's three
  lines of wiring. I'll verify it by hand in a browser and say so rather than claim coverage I don't have. Same for
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m's stub return, which is a literal.

